  "text": "To find the value of the determinant \\(\\begin{vmatrix} \\cos^2 \\alpha & \\cos \\alpha \\sin \\alpha & \\sin^2 \\alpha \\\\ \\cos^2 \\beta & \\cos \\beta \\sin \\beta & \\sin^2 \\beta \\\\ \\cos^2 \\gamma & \\cos \\gamma \\sin \\gamma & \\sin^2 \\gamma \\end{vmatrix}\\) where \\(\\alpha + \\beta + \\gamma = \\pi\\), we can leverages the trigonometric identities and properties of orthogonality.\n\n### Step 1: Simplify the non-diagonal elements\nNotice that we can rewrite the non-diagonal (\\(\\alpha\\)-\\(\\beta\\) and \\(\\alpha\\)-\\(\\gamma\\) elements).\n\n\\[\n\\cos \\alpha \\sin \\alpha = \\frac{1}{2} \\sin 2\\alpha\n\\]\n\\[\n\\cos \\beta \\sin \\beta = \\frac{1}{2} \\sin 2\\beta\n\\]\n\\[\n\\cos \\gamma \\sin \\gamma = \\frac{1}{2} \\sin 2\\gamma\n\\]\n\n### Step 2: Use orthogonality property\nSince \\(\\alpha + \\beta + \\gamma = \\pi\\), we can recognize this as a property of trigonometric functions. In particular, we use the fact that the orthogonality of trigonometric functions becomes significant when combined in a cyclic form. For three angles \\(\\alpha\\), \\(\\beta\\), and \\(\\gamma\\) whose sum is \\(\\pi\\), the determinant is zero due to the orthogonality property.\n\nThis property is a standard result in the context of trigonometric identities and geometry, and it implies that the determinant is zero.\n\n### Step 3: Conclude the determinant value\nGiven the orthogonality, we can conclude that the determinant is zero for the given angles \\(\\alpha\\), \\(\\beta\\), and \\(\\gamma\\).\n\n### Final Answer\n\\[\n\\boxed{0}\n\\]",
